Reading this table.
  /gp-writeto is the fair baseline on encode: CalculateSize + WriteTo(Span) into a
    reused buffer, no allocation. On decode the baseline column is gp-parse for both.
  /gp-tba is Google.Protobuf's ToByteArray, which is the call application code
    actually writes. The two differ by one allocation of the output, and the gap
    between the two columns is that allocation with nothing else in it.
  managed against managed-2pass is a WITHIN-ARM delta in the same rounds (R4): one
    pass with a learned length width against two passes with exact prefixes, which
    is the shape Google.Protobuf uses. It prices the single pass ALONE.
  memcpy floor is R2's bound: no encoder produces N bytes for less than one copy of
    N bytes. On DECODE it is a weak floor except on the M5 bulk rows, where a
    decode is a copy; elsewhere read it as a scale and not as a bound.
  ns/elem divides by the element count, which for P1.1 (4 elements) is a per-MESSAGE
    cost divided by four and is NOT comparable with P1.2's.
  There are no crossing counts here: every arm in this table is in-process managed
    code and crosses nothing. The counts R5 asks for belong to the core-ffi arm,
    which is not built.

